[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m ---
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m ### Step 2: Prime Factorization of $9!$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m Factorizing $9!$ into its prime components:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m - The exponent of 2: $ \lfloor 9/2 \rfloor + \lfloor 9/4 \rfloor + \lfloor 9/8 \rfloor = 4 + 2 + 1 = 7 $
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m - The exponent of 3: $ \lfloor 9/3 \rfloor + \lfloor 9/9 \rfloor = 3 + 1 = 4 $
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m - The exponent of 5: $ \lfloor 9/5 \rfloor = 1 $
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m - The exponent of 7: $ \lfloor 9/7 \rfloor = 1 $
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m So the prime factorization of $9!$ is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m 9! = 2^7 \times 3^4 \times 5^1 \times 7^1
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m ---
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m ### Step 3: Understand the Units Digit Condition
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m We are looking for divisors $d$ of $9!$ such that the unit digit of $d$ is 1. This is equivalent to $d \equiv 1 \mod 10$. 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m However, any number ending in 1 cannot be divisible by 2 or 5 (since:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m - Divisibility by 2 implies the number is even.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m - Divisibility by 5 implies the number ends in 0 or 5.)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m Thus, the candidate divisors must not be divisible by 2 or 5, meaning they must be of the form:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m d = 3^b \times 7^c
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m where $0 \leq b \leq 4$, $0 \leq c \leq 1$.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=50248)[0m This is because in the prime factorization of $9!$, the only exponents of 3 and 7 that matter for the divisors not divisible by 2 or 5 are within their respective limits.
